WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:07.750
Let's go.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.590
I did release a course on the Jebaba

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:13.730
London system with my good friend,

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.670
Grandmaster Oleksandr Bortnik.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.390
The link to that course will be in the

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.950
description to the next few YouTube

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.730
videos, including this one, and I'll

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.370
redirect you to the video where I kind

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:26.970
of advertise and preview the course

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.690
more specifically, so in a couple of

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.670
the subsequent games, I did want to

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.570
introduce the Jebaba London and show a

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.430
couple of cool lines from the course.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.710
So this will be sort of in parallel to

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.210
the main speed run recommendation,

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.650
which is of course 1E4, we had one

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.050
Jebaba London game already, and in that

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.970
game I did preview the course, this was

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.010
a few games ago, but anyways, let's

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.070
focus on the task at hand.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.710
We are facing the move 1G6, and when

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.310
you're a D4 player, you have to be

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.230
flexible if you want to be a

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.650
principled, serious opening player in

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.450
the sense that when you face moves like

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:08.130
G6 or D6 or E6, there's no dispute that

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.810
the best way to quote unquote punish

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.070
these openings is to play principled

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.250
chess, right, if you're a London

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.310
player, you're a D4, C4 player, you

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.390
know, if you want to really fight for

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.330
advantages in every game in principled

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.410
fashion, then investing some time

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.990
studying certain D4, E4 openings is

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.290
very much worthwhile, and this doesn't

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.490
necessarily make you an E4 player, this

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.730
doesn't mean that you, these openings

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.110
have nothing to do with the more

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.550
mainstream lines like the Sicilian and

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.350
the Ruy Lopez, and you can study them

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.190
from the white side, even if you're not

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.550
too familiar with ideas inherent in 1E4

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.870
openings, this I think is a big

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.130
misconception, which is that, let's say

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.130
you're a London player and you say,

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.810
well, if I play E4, I have to know the

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.610
ideas that attend to 1E4 openings, not

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.070
at all, this is its own thing, and you

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.690
can study this as its own thing, you

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.010
don't have to be an E4 player to crush

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.270
an opening like the modern

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.490
consistently, okay, and hopefully this

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.470
game is going to show that I am an E4

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.210
player, but I'm going to try to show

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.510
that these openings are pretty

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.910
approachable and you can learn them

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.410
without investing 30 hours into, you

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:18.570
know, learning an entire chessable

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.790
course or learning a ton of theory,

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.790
this is of course the modern, and we

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.730
have a wealth of options against the

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.190
modern starting from this position

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.130
where we can develop either the knight

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.170
to f3 or the knight to c3, I've also

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.270
previously played the move pawn to c3

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.830
in previous speedrun games in order to

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.870
take this bishop out of commission, but

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.210
let's play principal chess in this

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.950
game, let's go knight c3, which is of

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.710
course the main move, and it's also the

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.310
most flexible move because you reserve

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.690
the ability to push the pawn up to f4,

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.230
that is the Austrian attack, which is

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.130
the most principled way of meeting the

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.030
perk, but we're right now facing a

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.730
different system, this is called the

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.290
Gergenidze system, named after Georgian

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.190
grandmaster, Bukhudi Gergenidze, and

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.530
this has been around really since the

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.990
50s and 60s, Hikaru loves to play this

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.390
system with black and blitz games

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.610
because it's an easy system to play,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.830
and oftentimes the center gets really

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.750
locked up, so if white is unfamiliar

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.130
with the main ideas, you can really get

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:24.430
a feeling that this position is still

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.290
to play f4, but that's not what we're

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.810
going to do, I'm going to introduce you

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.290
to a very practical system against the

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.570
Gergenidze, which gives white a very

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.830
easy position to play, black can

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.490
equalize against this system with ultra

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:40.670
-precise play, but it's practical in the

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.430
sense that most players, I think under

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.990
24, 2300, will probably not be familiar

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.430
with the correct pathway and will not

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.470
reproduce it to the most precise

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.010
extent, what we do here is start with

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.470
the move bishop to e3, you can also

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.290
start with the move f3, I think bishop

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.650
e3 is more flexible, you want to

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.170
reserve the pawn moves and delay them

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.910
until you absolutely have to play them,

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.370
whereas you know that bishop e3 is a

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.710
nice developing move as it is, black is

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.970
not obliged to play d5, and I would

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.850
define the Gergenidze as a cross

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:18.570
between the Karokan and the modern, so

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.470
black tries to get the best of all

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.590
worlds, and the move bishop e3 is

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.870
helpful even if black deviates and goes

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.810
d6, and transitions back into like a

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.930
perk setup, bishop on e3 is a very,

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.470
very good piece, whereas we're not

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.490
totally sure that we in fact want to

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.470
put the pawn on f3, but now that black

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.710
has played d5, we basically are playing

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.750
the fantasy variation, my official

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.330
recommendation against the Karokan, as

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.410
you hopefully know, is the fantasy, so

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.030
if you strip away these two minor

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.390
pieces and this piece, we actually just

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.510
have a fantasy, and it's quite clear

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.270
that the moves knight c3 and bishop e3

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.490
have not detracted from the

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.570
effectiveness of our setup, to the

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.090
contrary, that's actually where the

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.390
bishop often belongs in the fantasy,

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.090
and the knight of course is usually

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.310
developed to c3 as well, so there's

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.110
only one move, as far as I know, that

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:12.930
allows black to fight for equality, all

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.590
other moves give white an excellent

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.270
position, and it's easy to lose this

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.110
very, very quickly if you're not ultra

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.210
accurate with black, a lot of players

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.630
just don't have a good sense of where

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.590
the pieces belong here, and one of the

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.670
biggest draws of the move f3 is that

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.950
black experiences problems with their

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.650
knight on g8, in the Gurganidze system,

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:37.010
you might have seen like Hikaru and

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.310
others bring the knight out to h6, here

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.230
this move is not sensible because white

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.630
plays queen d2, the f5 square is

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.450
protected, that's the key, that's why

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.830
we're delaying the move e5, meanwhile

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:49.770
if black plays knight f6, that also

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.870
walks into e5, and black has to lose

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.270
yet another tempo, shifting the knight

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.450
back toward a very awkward square, such

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.430
as d7, our opponent counterstrikes in

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.130
the center, I will admit that I'm not

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:02.470
familiar with this move, and perhaps

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.310
neither is our opponent, but this is a

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:08.670
move which isn't justified from a

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.570
general principle standpoint, because

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.210
we are the ones with a lead in

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.290
development, black is blasting open the

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:16.790
center, which should favor the side

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.010
that has more pieces developed, in this

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.010
case it's us, and I don't think that we

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.710
should overthink it, right, hey our

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.810
opponent wants to open up the center, I

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.030
am absolutely game, the one thing that

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.510
you should notice is that after pawn

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.290
takes e5, which I think is what we're

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.150
gonna play, I don't see the reason, I

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.610
don't particularly see a compelling

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.950
reason to take the pawn on d5, we can

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.270
do it on the next move, what you should

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.770
notice though is after d takes e, black

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.230
does not have the move d4, you should

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.630
make a mental note to yourself that

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.430
these pieces are unforkable squares,

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.510
but d4 doesn't work because we have two

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.650
defenders on that square, and black is

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.970
only one attacker, so I think the best

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.310
most flexible way to continue is first

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.570
and foremost to take on e5, you want to

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.230
open up the center, I'm more than happy

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.730
to do that, and if black recaptures

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.630
with a bishop, I don't see a reason

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.910
that we can't just then take on d5 and

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:08.930
hunt for the extra pawn.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:11.910
Alternatively, we could focus on

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.670
quickly developing our queen side with

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.490
queen d2 and long castle in order to

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.370
exert more pressure on the d5 pawn,

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:20.870
that leads to some interesting tactical

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.590
complications, queen d2 here may seem

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.130
to be a blunder due to d4, but if you

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.970
look a little bit further in that

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:29.730
position, we can castle queen side and

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.390
black can't move the d pawn because the

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:33.190
Queen's gonna hang with checkmate, but

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:35.570
black can support the d pawn by playing

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.950
c5, and I'm not totally convinced that

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.030
that's the way the way to go, I mean

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:44.010
other than e takes d5, perhaps there's

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.350
like bishop to d4, trying to get the

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.210
the dark squared bishops off the board

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.910
and really underscore the the weakness

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.170
of the dark squares on the King's side,

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.210
but that just seems a little bit too

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.130
fancy, I think that we should start

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.570
with a move e takes d5, and depending

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.230
on how black responds, we will either

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:05.190
try to solidify our extra material or

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.370
we will focus on getting our King out

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.470
of the center, because you should

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:11.310
remember that when you play the move

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.290
f3, you make rather serious weaknesses

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.130
in your center and on your King side,

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.530
in particular the bishop on e3 loses

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:20.650
its support, why is that important?

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:23.430
Well if black plays knight f6 and then

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.470
castles and puts a rook on e8, we're

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.070
gonna have to be really really careful

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:28.850
because that bishop on e3 is gonna get

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:30.870
caught on the crossfire, and the last

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.830
thing that you want is a King stuck in

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:35.510
the center after you've played the move

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.390
f3, and after blacks gotten a rook to

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.610
e8, so our biggest priority here, apart

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:42.110
from making sure that this pawn remains

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.910
healthy, is perhaps an even bigger

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.070
priority, is to go Queen d2 and long

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.370
castle and get the King to safety, so

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.950
that we can focus on operating properly

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.110
on the King side, hopefully that makes

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.850
sense, okay pawn takes d5, so this

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.510
poses us with a dilemma, do not play

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:01.990
Queen takes d5, that drops the Queen to

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.530
Bishop takes c3, because that is a

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:06.070
check, we could play Knight takes d5,

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.930
and in response I presume that black

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:10.850
wants to play Bishop takes b2, but

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:12.530
there's a move I like even more than

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:14.330
Knight takes d5, this pawn is now an

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.150
isolated pawn, technically it's an IQP,

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.110
this is not the usual IQP setups that

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.010
we tend to get, but it's still an IQP

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:24.990
structure, a pawn on d5 is a serious

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.610
weakness, so the move I am very tempted

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:30.650
to play is still Queen to d2, and in

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.690
contrast to playing it here, what's the

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.690
chief difference, well after Queen d2,

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.770
d4 long castle, black doesn't have

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.790
another pawn to defend d4 with, and if

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.330
black plays there the move Knight to

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.950
c6, who can tell me a way in which we

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:48.750
can indirectly reinforce the pressure

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.750
on the d4 pawn, I think that move, that

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.010
resulting move is super important, and

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:56.670
it's the move Bishop f1 to b5, and the

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.210
secondary point of that move is that if

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.810
black then takes on e3, we take on d8,

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.670
and the Knight on c6 is pinned, so

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.590
because of that line I think Queen d2,

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:08.350
it works out tactically, and so it just

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.330
makes a lot more sense to me to develop

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:13.010
quickly rather than trying to greedily

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.470
capture the pawn and get into tactical

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.230
complications after Bishop takes b2,

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.050
maybe those complications favor white,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:20.690
I don't know, but in this speedrun

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.370
we're trying to play simple

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:23.510
straightforward chess whenever

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.390
possible, so again the line that we had

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:29.530
to calculate is d4, long castle, black

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:32.270
can't take because the Queen hangs, and

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:34.410
Knight c6 is always met with Bishop b5,

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:35.930
okay our opponent develops himself,

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.290
Knight e7, I think we continue with our

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:40.510
plan, we long castle anyway, and we

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.150
exert additional pressure on the d5

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:45.890
pawn, what you should note here is that

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:49.310
our advantage is not just based on the

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:51.150
weakness of the d5 pawn, it's not like

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.410
if we don't win that pawn then the

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.310
position is equal, we're also better

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.270
from a positional standpoint because we

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.770
have control of the square in front of

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:01.790
the pawn, and as you know from previous

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.750
discussions of IQP structures, that's

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.630
like the first thing that you should be

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.350
paying attention to, who's got this

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:10.150
control of the square in front of the

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.590
isolated Queen pawn, if it's us then we

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.490
can put a Knight on that square, and

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:17.070
that's a pretty juicy Knight, let alone

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.350
