00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:52.650
be made for taking on e6 with the Rook,

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:54.850
in the sense that it keeps the

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.330
immediate pressure on the Knight on e7,

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:58.930
and it does threaten to win a second

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:00.570
pawn, we're actually threatening there

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.250
to take on c6, force the Knight to

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.590
recapture, and then to take the pawn on

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:07.190
d5, I don't really want to part ways

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:09.370
with my Bishop if I can help it, but it

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.690
will be worth paying that price for a

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:13.950
second pawn, what worries me slightly

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:15.870
after Rook takes c6 is a weird move

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.070
like Knight f5, and then the other

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:19.790
Knight is threatening to jump into d4,

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:21.370
there's like fork ideas, and in the

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.350
interest of time, I'm also trying to

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.710
play in a way that corresponds with the

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:26.890
amount of time we have left, Knight

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.790
takes c6 is just a simpler move to

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:31.410
handle, right, generally in the endgame

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.350
you want your Rooks operating from a

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:35.230
distance, you don't want them on the

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:36.770
front lines because they start, you

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:37.770
know what I'm talking about, right,

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.590
when you have a Rook that's battling

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:42.730
minor pieces at close quarters, I have

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:44.690
a million examples of this, there's

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:46.890
more tactics, there are more forks, and

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.850
you run the risk of, you know, walking

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:51.690
into something like an ill -timed Knight d4.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:53.650
What about Knight f7?

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.430
Knight f7 is reasonable, but I didn't

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:58.530
see the point of that, okay, a6, where

00:22:58.530 --> 00:22:59.290
does our Bishop go?

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.450
Well, our main priority is to attack

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:03.290
the d5 pawn, the moment you tell this

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:04.850
to yourself, the move should become

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.170
very obvious, you have to play Bishop

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:08.570
a4 because the Bishop can later drop

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:12.570
back to b3 and exert direct pressure on d5.

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:15.590
Yeah, Bishop a4, Bishop b3 is just an

00:23:15.590 --> 00:23:17.310
El Classico kind of maneuver.

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:20.410
What's great about the placement of the

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:21.730
Knight on e6 is that it's also

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:24.190
controlling the d8 square, so on top of

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:25.910
everything else that Black is

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:29.210
experiencing, he can't put a Rook on

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:32.570
d8, the Knights are totally immobilized

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:34.530
as well, we're attacking d5 so the

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:35.650
other Knight can't move, the Knight

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.870
from c6 can move, where will it go?

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:41.010
Its mobility in the center is greatly

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.810
restricted, Black can barely move here.

00:23:44.110 --> 00:23:45.490
You might be sitting there with the

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.470
white pieces thinking, man, like, I

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:48.550
don't know if I'll be able to convert

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.810
this, I'm getting nervous, but if you

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:52.230
put yourself in your opponent's shoes,

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:54.350
Black literally is just like out of

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:57.330
moves, so however nervous you might

00:23:57.330 --> 00:23:58.710
feel, it's generally going to be

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:00.130
infinitely more difficult to be your

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:01.950
opponent in these types of situations

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:04.230
than it is to be the side that converts

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:04.910
the advantage.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:08.310
Another good move to just throw in, if

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:09.790
we have the time, and we certainly have

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:11.930
the time, not because Black has no

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:13.690
counterplays, is just to make some lift.

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:15.910
We can do that with the move c3, which

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:16.950
kills two birds with one stone.

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:19.510
It also fully immobilizes the d5 pawn,

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:21.910
making sure that d4 isn't going to

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:23.450
happen, it's harder to shoot at a

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:25.590
moving target, or of course we can play

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.270
the more straightforward a3.

00:24:27.910 --> 00:24:29.710
And if we had like 30 seconds at this

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:31.810
point, I would definitely play a3, right?

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.370
The less time you have, the more you

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:36.130
have to ensure yourself against the

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:38.170
possibility of some freak tactic that

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:39.450
you might not have the time to see.

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:41.710
So if this was a bullet game, I would

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:43.790
definitely be playing a3 on the next move.

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:47.770
Now how do I know that we should play a

00:24:47.770 --> 00:24:48.350
move like a3?

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:50.510
Well, it's all about whether or not

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:51.950
your opponent has counterplay, right?

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:54.530
Does your opponent have a clear path

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:58.430
that he can follow in order to generate counterplay?

00:24:58.470 --> 00:24:59.730
If the answer is yes, then you're

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:01.810
generally in a bit more of a hurry.

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.490
Here the answer is no, because black

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:06.870
has no squares for any of their pieces.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:08.290
They should back to b3.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:10.930
Okay, that's where we wanted to go

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:13.530
anyway, but there was nothing better.

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:14.650
Rook e8 is a good move.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:17.790
That's a tricky move, because if we

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.510
take on d5, then black will recapture

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:22.490
with the knight, we take with the rook,

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:26.050
and our knight on e6 there is a little

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:26.970
bit awkward, right?

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:28.870
Because it's pinned to the rook on e1

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:29.890
that's going to lose its defender.

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:32.750
So I don't want anything to have to do

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:33.830
with bishop takes d5.

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:34.670
There's no rush.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:37.310
That pawn isn't going anywhere at all.

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:39.070
So let's focus on improving our

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:41.530
position such that when we take the

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:42.930
pawn, we won't have to deal with a

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:46.010
fallout, and we won't have to rely on

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:47.890
tactics to get our knight out of e6.

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:50.170
The move that I mentioned earlier, I

00:25:50.170 --> 00:25:51.550
think it's a perfect time to play it.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:52.630
Let's go c3.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:55.470
Wait, actually let me think about this

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:57.270
for a second, because c3 maybe he's got

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:57.890
knight a5.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:25:59.350
Oh, but then we've got knight g7.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:00.490
That's beautiful.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:01.490
C3.

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:04.230
Yeah, I love the move c3, because

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:08.210
another thing to notice, and you notice

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:11.330
these things by avoiding tunnel vision, right?

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:13.290
It's easy to tunnel in on the d5 pawn

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.150
and forget that there are other tactics

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:18.270
that could have nothing to do with the

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:19.030
pawn on c5.

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:21.430
Anytime there's contact between pieces,

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:23.410
you have to notice that the rook on e1

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:26.010
is not making direct contact with the

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.650
knight on e7, but it could be making

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:30.110
direct contact after a certain

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:31.210
something move.

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:33.370
So we're just going to take on b4, and

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:35.190
I played that move quickly to lure my

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.110
opponent into recapturing quickly,

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:39.430
which I think he might have smelled a

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:41.350
rat, but at this point it's too late.

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:42.230
No, he didn't.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:45.130
And by not tunnel visioning, you are

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:46.890
going to be able to find a move that

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:47.970
wins on the spot.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.930
Now the knight on e7 is what we call a

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.490
type two undefended piece.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.810
It's only protected by one other piece.

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:56.210
If you can dislodge that rook from e8,

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.110
well then likely you're going to win

00:26:58.110 --> 00:26:59.310
the knight on e7, because the rook

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.490
can't maintain contact with the knight.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:02.830
There's no ninth rank.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:03.930
What's the move?

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:05.990
It's not bishop a4.

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:06.590
I tricked you.

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:08.250
Some of you might say, oh, it's bishop a4.

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.150
No, because then the rook moves, and

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:12.370
the white rook can't jump over the knight.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:14.350
You need to do two things at once.

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:16.430
You need to move the knight away and

00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:16.930
attack the rook.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:17.730
Can you do that?

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:19.410
You absolutely can, and that's the move

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:21.010
knight g7, fianchettoing the knight,

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:22.730
and that ends the game.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:24.030
That ends the game.

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:27.910
Yeah, knight g7 is one of those like

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:29.630
geometric moves that can be hard to

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:32.190
spot, because your brain doesn't often

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:34.510
associate like the g7 square for your

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:36.070
opponent with like a knight being

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:38.530
there, but the more concrete your

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:42.510
thinking, the more specifically you

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:44.330
formulate questions to yourself, the

00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:47.230
likelier it is that you see a

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.010
relatively unorthodox tactic, and

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:52.370
obviously like making observations does

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.130
not guarantee that you will find a tactic.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:56.390
It raises the probability that you see

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.550
stuff, and it raises the amount of

00:27:58.550 --> 00:27:59.690
things that you see.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:01.850
So by not tunneling in on the d5 pawn,

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:02.930
we were able to find this move.

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:05.410
If we had tunneled in on the d5 pawn,

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:07.570
we would have certainly played the move a3.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.930
That is the move I would play if I was

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:11.410
just obsessed with winning this pawn.

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:12.910
I'd say, oh, well, let's get knight out

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.490
of b4, and then let's take the pawn.

00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:17.310
Obviously knight g7 is quite a bit sexier.

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:19.090
Let's take the rook, and what's ironic

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:21.610
is that now the second idea comes into play.

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:22.290
This is awesome.

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:24.770
First, we win one rook by playing

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:25.450
knight g7.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:27.990
Now we can try to win the second one by

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:29.290
playing the move that some of you

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:30.250
wanted to play initially.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.430
Now it's time for bishop to a4.

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:34.630
I love that the bishop dropped back to

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:37.310
b3 in order to attack d5, and now moves

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:41.010
up again to a4, where it encounters

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:42.070
greener pastures.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:43.310
The game is over.

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:45.270
If black blocks with knight c6, we of

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:47.770
course simply take the knight, and then

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:50.030
we take on e8, and the opening

00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:51.370
experiment was a success.

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:53.950
So as we get into the analysis, once

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:56.530
again, the link to my Jebaba London

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:58.190
course is going to be in the

00:28:58.190 --> 00:28:59.990
description of this video, as is going

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:04.050
to be the link to a preview video that

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:05.750
tells you all you need to know about

00:29:05.950 --> 00:29:06.750
the course.

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:09.390
But in this game, it was not meant to

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:12.670
be, and we deviated on move one.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:14.350
Of course, after d4, black is not

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:17.130
obliged to play the moves d5 or knight f6.

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:19.190
That's what you're going to face 90 % of

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:21.050
the time, but you have to have

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:22.810
responses prepared against some of the

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:24.690
other moves in this position.

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:28.850
I'm talking f5, e6, b6, and you can

00:29:28.850 --> 00:29:32.070
learn most of those in a couple of well

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:32.570
-allocated days.

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:35.550
The Jebaba London itself happens after

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:37.910
knight f6, knight c3, d5, and bishop f4.

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:40.790
This is the starting position of the course.

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:43.650
Okay, so in this game, we faced the

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:44.410
move g6.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:46.330
This is, of course, the modern defense,

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:48.930
and I would call this the close sibling

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:49.870
of the Pirates.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:52.310
The Pirates, or the Pirque, as some of

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:55.050
us call it, is d6, of course.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:57.670
White plays generally e4, knight f6,

00:29:58.130 --> 00:29:59.310
knight c3, and g6.

00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:01.610
The modern is, I think, called that

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:04.090
because black delays the development of

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:05.150
the knight, and this is a somewhat

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:07.730
cagier version of the Pirates.

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:08.970
You might say, well, what's the utility

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.230
of delaying the move knight f6?

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.930
Well, by delaying this move, black

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:16.570
opens up more possibilities for themselves.

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:18.870
So, for example, after e4, bishop g7,

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:21.290
knight c3, okay, our opponent played

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:21.810
the Gorgonidze.

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:23.750
That's one thing you can't really do

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:25.710
with the Pirates because you already

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:26.630
have a pawn on d6.

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:28.210
So, this is one thing.

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:31.730
Another very common continuation that's

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:34.370
unique to the modern, after d6, I was

00:30:34.370 --> 00:30:36.770
planning to play f4 and go into the

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:37.530
Austrian attack.

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:39.570
Here, you can transpose into the

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:41.370
Pirates with knight f6, but you can

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:44.190
also play a move that, I think, is

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.110
sometimes known as Tiger's Modern,

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:48.550
named after a Swedish GM, Tiger Hillar Persson.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:50.630
If you're interested in exploring this

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.810
line from black's perspective, he wrote

00:30:52.810 --> 00:30:55.330
a book called Tiger's Modern that I've

00:30:55.330 --> 00:30:56.510
heard really good things about.

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:59.390
So, this is a serious opening for black.

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.570
The reason I don't recommend it at all

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:05.490
to anybody under, say, 2200 is because

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:07.130
you need to know reams of opening

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:09.030
theory, and you need to know it in

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:10.950
order to avoid getting completely crushed.

00:31:11.110 --> 00:31:13.430
So, this opening guarantees,

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:15.710
essentially, that you will have winning

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:17.370
chances, but it also guarantees that

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.470
you have quite a few losing chances.

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:21.310
So, I won't talk about a6 here because

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:24.130
we have plenty to discuss in the

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:25.250
context of the Gurgonidze.

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:28.230
So, c6 enters the Gurgonidze.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:29.970
I actually am curious whether

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:33.030
Gurgonidze was the first player to play

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:33.670
the move c6.

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:36.690
I'm going to bet no, and I'm going to

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:37.970
ask you guys a trivia question.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:41.190
Let's say the question is as follows.

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:43.330
Do we think that this position occurred

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:48.090
for the first time before 1900 or after 1900?

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:50.970
I have a guess, but I'm curious what

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:51.630
people think.

00:31:52.850 --> 00:31:54.510
When did this position first occur in

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:55.230
tournament practice?

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:56.570
And if you're watching on YouTube, you

00:31:56.570 --> 00:31:58.410
can try to make a guess.

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.170
Yeah, we're pretty much split down the middle.

