And here we reach a slightly better

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:06.130
version of this endgame for Black, but

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:06.970
White is still better.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:10.370
Takes, takes, knight to d7, we play e6,

00:42:10.790 --> 00:42:12.590
f takes e6, and you can play a move

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:15.330
like bishop to e2.

00:42:15.350 --> 00:42:15.610
Okay?

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:18.130
And after Black castles, the engine

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:20.670
actually likes keeping this bishop on

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:21.890
this diagonal because there are some

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:22.970
important squares on it.

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:25.490
You don't want to go bishop g3 and walk

00:42:25.490 --> 00:42:26.510
into knight e3, right?

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:27.890
So you want to keep the bishop here.

00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:29.030
You're going to castle.

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:31.030
You're going to enjoy your life in an

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:32.150
endgame like this.

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:35.390
So that's an overview of pawn takes e4.

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:37.570
Other moves aren't really worth talking

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:39.310
about other than the best move, which

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:40.630
is queen to b6.

00:42:41.050 --> 00:42:42.830
I'll get to it in a moment.

00:42:43.430 --> 00:42:45.230
If Black plays knight f6, you get like

00:42:45.230 --> 00:42:48.130
a much improved version of the

00:42:48.130 --> 00:42:49.910
Gorgonese because here, the knight

00:42:49.910 --> 00:42:51.390
can't easily get to f5.

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:53.090
So this position just sucks for Black.

00:42:53.530 --> 00:42:55.410
And you don't have to play f4, but I

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:56.210
think this makes sense.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:57.610
And then you're going to go knight f3,

00:42:57.990 --> 00:42:59.570
and potentially you're just going to go

00:42:59.570 --> 00:43:02.970
like h4 and blast through on the king side.

00:43:03.730 --> 00:43:06.310
So the best move, and the move that not

00:43:06.310 --> 00:43:07.890
a lot of people know at an intermediate

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:09.250
level, is queen to b6.

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:11.710
I had a game here, a classical game,

00:43:11.770 --> 00:43:13.930
against Alex Lenderman, who was a top

00:43:13.930 --> 00:43:18.030
American GM in 2011, which I managed to win.

00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:20.710
And the best move for White is just to

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:22.650
ignore the threat on b2 and play the

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:23.550
move queen to d2.

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:27.270
Black has to know to accept the sacrifice.

00:43:27.270 --> 00:43:28.970
It's not really a sacrifice because

00:43:28.970 --> 00:43:29.910
you're going to win the pawn back.

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:34.090
But as a sales pitch for this line, I

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:35.930
want to show you the start of my game

00:43:35.930 --> 00:43:36.990
against Lenderman to show you how

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:39.970
difficult it is to handle this type of

00:43:39.970 --> 00:43:41.270
position for Black.

00:43:41.350 --> 00:43:42.730
Let me find the game in the database.

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:44.630
Oh no, I'm sorry.

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:46.070
It was slightly different.

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:49.750
It was e4, g6, d4, bishop g7, knight

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:51.410
c3, c6, bishop e3.

00:43:51.730 --> 00:43:53.690
And he immediately played the move

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:54.190
queen b6.

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:55.410
And this is a different version.

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:58.530
I played queen d2, so I was almost right.

00:43:58.950 --> 00:44:02.490
Takes rook b1, queen a3, knight f3.

00:44:02.830 --> 00:44:04.310
And this position is an improved

00:44:04.310 --> 00:44:06.510
version for White, because Black can't

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.470
play d5 anymore.

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:08.970
That square is now covered twice.

00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:10.490
But watch what happened in the

00:44:10.490 --> 00:44:11.070
Lenderman game.

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:14.210
He played the move b5 in order to

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:17.010
prevent the bishop from coming out to c4.

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:20.690
Who can tell me how I punished his lack

00:44:20.690 --> 00:44:21.510
of development here?

00:44:22.390 --> 00:44:24.770
And there's a beautiful detail, a

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:25.870
really nice move that would have

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:27.610
finished the game immediately that I missed.

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:30.290
Here, the move, like, plays itself.

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:31.750
Like, you just can't resist, of course.

00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:32.590
It's bishop b5.

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:34.270
And Lenderman saw this.

00:44:34.930 --> 00:44:35.950
He accepts my bishop here.

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:37.270
I started to get very excited.

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:39.350
And this is the position that he was

00:44:39.350 --> 00:44:40.070
aiming for.

00:44:40.810 --> 00:44:42.910
I missed the best move.

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:43.950
I played knight c7.

00:44:44.230 --> 00:44:45.970
And after king d8, we had total and

00:44:45.970 --> 00:44:46.630
complete chaos.

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:48.970
White, however, is winning on the spot

00:44:48.970 --> 00:44:50.870
with an incredible move.

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:52.050
This is really beautiful.

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:53.850
Let's see if anybody can find it.

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:54.970
Pause the video if you're on YouTube.

00:44:55.370 --> 00:44:56.870
Maybe take a good, like, three to five

00:44:56.870 --> 00:44:58.610
minutes here if you really want to work

00:44:58.610 --> 00:44:59.470
your tactical brain.

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:01.130
Not castles.

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:02.270
That's a little slow.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:04.210
Castles is slow.

00:45:05.550 --> 00:45:06.690
No, it's not castles.

00:45:06.870 --> 00:45:08.750
You see, if you castle, then Black will

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:11.530
defend the c7 square with knight a6.

00:45:11.590 --> 00:45:12.690
And you say, oh, I trapped the queen.

00:45:12.850 --> 00:45:13.390
You don't.

00:45:13.610 --> 00:45:14.330
The queen has c4.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:15.890
And that's a hint.

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:19.350
C4 is the only evacuation square that

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:19.910
the queen has.

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:20.670
You got it.

00:45:21.030 --> 00:45:22.150
Sardine, you earned a sub.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:24.230
Or everybody who answered is a sub.

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:26.450
Queen to c3 ends the game immediately.

00:45:26.870 --> 00:45:29.650
Because if Black takes with check, you

00:45:29.650 --> 00:45:30.510
bring the king up.

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:31.810
Black loses the queen.

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:34.310
Because if he takes, you get checkmated

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:34.850
on c8.

00:45:34.910 --> 00:45:36.190
Now, this is a very high -level move.

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:39.710
If Black plays knight to a6 in order to

00:45:39.710 --> 00:45:41.630
defend the bishop, you win the game

00:45:41.630 --> 00:45:42.770
with the secondary idea.

00:45:42.950 --> 00:45:43.670
Rook to a1.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:45.630
There's no more c4 square.

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:47.930
And queen e6 runs into rook takes a6.

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:49.730
And Black loses everything.

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:51.230
Actually, Black loses the queen because

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:51.830
of knight c7.

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:53.890
Now, the game could last a little bit

00:45:53.890 --> 00:45:55.350
longer because Black has a rook and a

00:45:55.350 --> 00:45:55.970
piece for the queen.

00:45:56.030 --> 00:45:57.110
But it's, like, plus six.

00:45:57.490 --> 00:45:59.430
Obviously, White's just crushing.

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:03.310
So this just gives you a sense of how

00:46:03.310 --> 00:46:04.710
difficult these positions are to play.

00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:06.970
But unfortunately, we're talking here

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:08.870
about a slightly different version.

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:11.050
Here, with the pawn on d5, it's improved.

00:46:11.930 --> 00:46:13.810
But after queen takes b2, rook b1,

00:46:13.910 --> 00:46:14.310
queen a3.

00:46:14.390 --> 00:46:15.090
Actually, I was wrong.

00:46:16.370 --> 00:46:18.730
Yeah, because in the Lenderman game, my

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:19.530
knight was on f3.

00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:21.090
So that's the main reason this is an

00:46:21.090 --> 00:46:22.630
improved version for Black.

00:46:22.990 --> 00:46:24.310
Here, you play e takes d5.

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:28.350
And I still maintain that this is a

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:30.530
much easier position to play for White.

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:31.310
But it is equal.

00:46:31.490 --> 00:46:33.590
And it's equal if Black knows the move

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:34.950
knight to f6, first of all.

00:46:35.750 --> 00:46:38.670
You bring the bishop out, I'm pretty

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:40.090
sure, according to what I looked at.

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:41.510
No, you play d takes c6.

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:43.490
And here, Black has to know another move.

00:46:43.570 --> 00:46:46.050
Knight takes c6 is a bad move because

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:46.750
of d5.

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:47.890
And White gets a big initiative.

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:51.250
You have to know the move bc, which is

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:52.730
far from intuitive, I think.

00:46:53.510 --> 00:46:55.250
White develops the bishop to d3.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:57.270
Castles, knight g2.

00:46:57.390 --> 00:46:58.990
White is a very compact position.

00:46:59.690 --> 00:47:01.650
And again, engine gives equal, but

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:03.290
Black has to know more engine moves

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:04.350
like bishop to a6.

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.370
And finally, this position is equal for

00:47:07.370 --> 00:47:08.990
obvious reasons.

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:10.650
I mean, Black has good, well -placed

00:47:10.650 --> 00:47:11.790
pieces, but so does White.

00:47:12.130 --> 00:47:13.310
So it's one of those things.

00:47:13.410 --> 00:47:14.690
If your opponent knows all of these

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:16.730
moves, if you watch this video, kudos.

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:18.010
You know, take your hat off.

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:19.870
Sometimes you just have to admit that

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:20.910
your opponent knows everything.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:22.370
But I don't think that you're going to

00:47:22.370 --> 00:47:24.950
face this sequence of moves all that often.

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:26.650
You're going to face knight f6 or pawn

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:27.210
takes e4.

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:28.950
Some people are going to probably go

00:47:28.950 --> 00:47:31.010
knight h6, which is really dumb because

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:33.310
of queen d2 sending the knight right

00:47:33.310 --> 00:47:34.150
back to g8.

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:35.790
Okay.

00:47:35.910 --> 00:47:37.670
So finally, we get to our opponent's

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:39.070
move, which is nothing to sneeze at.

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:41.390
I mean, e5 is a legitimate alternative,

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:43.970
but I'm pretty sure that it hands over

00:47:43.970 --> 00:47:46.390
a big initiative and a big advantage

00:47:46.390 --> 00:47:47.830
after pawn takes e5.

00:47:48.510 --> 00:47:48.690
Okay.

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:49.790
So bishop takes e5.

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:52.410
And we played the move e takes d5,

00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:55.470
which is normal according to the computer.

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:57.370
The computer also likes the immediate

00:47:57.370 --> 00:47:59.710
bishop to d4, which came across our radar.

00:47:59.870 --> 00:48:01.590
I mean, this is not an engine move.

00:48:01.690 --> 00:48:04.130
It's a pretty obvious idea.

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:06.870
And according to the computer, forcing

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:09.030
this bishop off the board would give

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:11.250
White a big advantage, like plus 1 .5.

00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:14.110
And in my move, it's around plus 0 .8.

00:48:14.390 --> 00:48:16.330
So it's not a mistake, but bishop d4 is

00:48:16.330 --> 00:48:17.190
even more accurate.

00:48:17.690 --> 00:48:19.190
What was I worried about here?

00:48:19.290 --> 00:48:20.730
I thought that Black would go here.

00:48:21.470 --> 00:48:23.910
But apparently, after you trade, you

00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:25.810
just go queen d2, and White's huge

00:48:25.810 --> 00:48:28.610
development advantage gives him just an

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:29.570
overwhelming position.

00:48:29.910 --> 00:48:31.830
Wherever Black goes, you're now going

00:48:31.830 --> 00:48:32.190
to castle.

00:48:32.410 --> 00:48:32.570
Ah.

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:35.250
And Black can't take on e4 because of

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:36.130
the mate.

00:48:36.710 --> 00:48:38.910
And if Black castles, then you say,

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:40.030
thank you very much for the pawn.

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:40.790
Takes, takes, takes.

00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:42.170
And White is much better.

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:44.910
So bishop d4 would have been best, but

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:46.610
instead, we play d takes d5, which I

00:48:46.610 --> 00:48:48.270
think is perfectly suitable.

00:48:49.050 --> 00:48:49.950
C takes d5.

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.370
And of course, we bring our queen up to d2.

00:48:53.650 --> 00:48:56.590
And White has a small -ish but very

00:48:56.590 --> 00:48:57.470
stable advantage.

00:48:57.590 --> 00:48:59.170
And most importantly, this is a much

00:48:59.170 --> 00:49:01.470
easier position to play for White.

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:05.450
So hopefully, that overview was

00:49:05.450 --> 00:49:07.690
sensible, and you understood all of the

00:49:07.690 --> 00:49:08.210
main ideas.

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:10.570
So here we develop our pieces, 97,

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:12.070
castles, bishop e6.

00:49:12.190 --> 00:49:14.330
I think this is quite normal and

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:17.410
obvious without commentary.

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:19.970
The next interesting moment really

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:22.870
occurred after we played 94, Black

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:23.550
played queen d7.

00:49:23.730 --> 00:49:25.330
And I feel like Black embarks

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:27.430
fundamentally on the wrong plan.

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:29.010
I just feel like castling queenside

00:49:29.010 --> 00:49:32.230
here opens Black up to so many problems

00:49:32.230 --> 00:49:34.370
in the center and on the queenside.

00:49:34.490 --> 00:49:37.030
I would go so far as to say that at a

00:49:37.030 --> 00:49:39.130
title player level, castling queenside

00:49:39.130 --> 00:49:41.050
might be close to the decisive mistake.

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:43.570
What would I do instead here for Black?

00:49:43.670 --> 00:49:45.410
Well, the move that concerned me the

00:49:45.410 --> 00:49:47.150
most was actually a very simple

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:49.090
approach where Black trades off all the

00:49:49.090 --> 00:49:51.750
pieces that he can and castles kingside.

00:49:51.830 --> 00:49:53.970
And I did mention during the game that

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:56.470
piece trades favor the side that plays

00:49:56.470 --> 00:49:57.450
against the IQP.

00:49:57.910 --> 00:49:59.870
Well, yes, but here Black has bigger

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:00.570
fish to fry.

00:50:00.650 --> 00:50:01.990
In particular, Black wants to avoid

00:50:01.990 --> 00:50:03.230
getting checkmated.

00:50:03.770 --> 00:50:05.750
And the queen on d4 is not a good

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:06.730
blockader, right?

00:50:06.810 --> 00:50:08.910
By trading, you've essentially lured

00:50:08.910 --> 00:50:11.330
the queen onto d4 where it can be

00:50:11.330 --> 00:50:13.030
susceptible to, of course, the move

00:50:13.030 --> 00:50:14.850
knight f5 or to the move knight c6.

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:17.470
And if White plays h4, Black can play

00:50:17.470 --> 00:50:18.190
knight c6.

00:50:18.590 --> 00:50:20.610
And if we play queen d2, Black can push d4.

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:22.490
And it's quite clear that Black has

00:50:22.490 --> 00:50:25.370
generated serious counterplay against

00:50:25.370 --> 00:50:26.010
the queenside.

00:50:26.110 --> 00:50:27.570
According to the engine, White is still

00:50:27.570 --> 00:50:29.350
quite a bit better with knight e4

00:50:29.350 --> 00:50:32.730
because queen d5 runs into the fork and

00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:36.590
bishop takes a2 is very dubious on

00:50:36.590 --> 00:50:38.570
account of the typical b3.

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:40.650
So White is still better.

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:44.030
But Black's position remains quite playable.

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:46.330
Maybe this is a better option after

00:50:46.330 --> 00:50:47.310
queen d2, d4.

00:50:47.830 --> 00:50:49.950
Here, this comes with far greater

00:50:49.950 --> 00:50:52.350
effect because the knight can get into e3.

00:50:53.230 --> 00:50:55.290
So something like this, I think, would

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:57.130
have given Black a defensible position

00:50:57.130 --> 00:51:00.050
after queen d7, bishop b5, long castle.

00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:03.750
And king b1 is maybe less to the point

00:51:03.750 --> 00:51:05.050
than a move like rook he1.

00:51:05.130 --> 00:51:06.910
But I just wanted to illustrate how

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:09.050
pleasant positions like this are to

00:51:09.050 --> 00:51:10.490
play and how you really just need to

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:12.790
kind of enjoy positions like this.

00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:15.450
And moves like king b1 signify that

00:51:15.450 --> 00:51:18.310
you're not in any massive hurry to do

00:51:18.310 --> 00:51:19.010
something crazy.

00:51:19.010 --> 00:51:21.570
Just make these improving moves, and

00:51:21.570 --> 00:51:23.710
you will notice how your opponent often

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:25.170
just collapses on their own.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:28.510
King b8, and now a very important move,

00:51:28.610 --> 00:51:30.870
bishop f4, trading off our opponent's

00:51:30.870 --> 00:51:31.690
strongest piece.

00:51:32.010 --> 00:51:33.050
Was this the only move?

00:51:33.110 --> 00:51:33.370
No.

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:35.830
Probably the computer would prefer

00:51:35.830 --> 00:51:37.290
something more straightforward like

00:51:37.290 --> 00:51:37.830
rook he1.

00:51:38.390 --> 00:51:42.750
But it's a very sensible move because

00:51:42.750 --> 00:51:45.310
we are leaving Black with a terrible

00:51:45.310 --> 00:51:47.910
bishop on e6 that's essentially just a

00:51:47.910 --> 00:51:48.390
big pawn.

00:51:49.010 --> 00:51:50.690
So bishop f4, queen f4, and I think

00:51:50.690 --> 00:51:52.950
another mistake is the move king a8.

00:51:53.030 --> 00:51:55.930
I think queen c7 should have been

00:51:55.930 --> 00:51:57.930
preferred, essentially forcing our hand.

00:51:58.030 --> 00:51:59.750
Our opponent gave us too many free moves.

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:02.330
He let us bring all our pieces to great squares.

00:52:02.590 --> 00:52:04.110
I mean, it's no surprise then that we

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:05.150
had a game -winning tactic.

00:52:05.570 --> 00:52:08.010
Literally all of our pieces are located

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:09.410
on perfect squares.

00:52:09.730 --> 00:52:11.610
When that is the case, you just

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:13.330
automatically need to search for tactics.

00:52:13.330 --> 00:52:14.730
If you're looking at your position,

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:15.390
you're saying, wait a second,

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:17.290
everything is placed perfectly.

00:52:17.590 --> 00:52:20.390
It's all staring at crucial files and weaknesses.

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:22.510
You better start looking for tactics.

00:52:23.170 --> 00:52:26.090
Okay, so queen c7, I think, was Black's

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:28.310
last chance to attain a defensible position.

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:31.650
I might even have taken on c7 and taken

00:52:31.650 --> 00:52:33.950
on c6, something like this.

00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:36.390
The engine actually likes this because

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:40.010
after bc, rook he1, again, Black is

00:52:40.010 --> 00:52:41.910
under tremendous pressure on the e

00:52:41.910 --> 00:52:45.390
-file, and rook to d6 is possible, but

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:47.410
now we can target the square on c5.

00:52:48.230 --> 00:52:50.210
Whenever you have a structure that

00:52:50.210 --> 00:52:53.250
involves hanging pawns on c6 and d5,

00:52:53.850 --> 00:52:56.510
your sort of traditional textbook plan

00:52:56.510 --> 00:52:58.690
involves what I call the Rubinstein

00:52:58.690 --> 00:53:01.710
maneuver, and I've named it after Akiba

00:53:01.710 --> 00:53:05.170
Rubinstein, who demonstrated a perfect

00:53:05.170 --> 00:53:08.750
way to play against this pawn chain, c6

00:53:08.750 --> 00:53:09.270
and d5.

00:53:09.410 --> 00:53:11.050
What idea am I talking about?

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:13.350
What square are we trying to occupy,

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:15.230
and with what piece, and for what purpose?

00:53:15.430 --> 00:53:17.030
But if you want, you can just name the square.

00:53:17.850 --> 00:53:19.750
So don't touch the knight on d4.

00:53:19.850 --> 00:53:20.330
It's perfect.

00:53:20.450 --> 00:53:21.850
The knight on d4 is serving an

00:53:21.850 --> 00:53:23.830
important purpose, but the other knight

00:53:23.830 --> 00:53:26.990
is unemployed, and we want to go knight

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:28.170
a4 and knight c5.

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:29.970
By occupying the second blockading

00:53:29.970 --> 00:53:32.390
square, we're completely immobilizing

00:53:32.390 --> 00:53:33.390
this pawn chain.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:35.850
It's the same principle as occupying

00:53:35.850 --> 00:53:38.550
the d4 or d5 square when one side has

00:53:38.550 --> 00:53:39.190
an IQP.

00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:43.250
Here is a very quick run through of the

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:44.930
Rubinstein game that I'm talking about.

00:53:45.470 --> 00:53:47.810
Thing is, these two players, Rubinstein

00:53:47.810 --> 00:53:50.490
and his opponent was Georg Salve, who

00:53:50.490 --> 00:53:52.670
was one of the top players of that time.

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:55.050
Yeah, basically what happened in this

00:53:55.050 --> 00:53:57.870
game was a situation in which Black got

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:02.130
that structure, okay, and White did

00:54:02.210 --> 00:54:03.590
exactly what we just discussed.

00:54:03.750 --> 00:54:05.310
White brings his pieces out, knight a4,

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:07.890
there it is, and White piles up on the

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:09.570
c5 square with everything that he's got.

00:54:09.670 --> 00:54:14.130
Rook c1, bishop c5, there it is, and

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:16.150
everything is all about the c5 square.

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:18.090
By keeping this square under control,

00:54:18.230 --> 00:54:20.410
you're ensuring that c6 remains a

00:54:20.410 --> 00:54:21.910
weakness forever, and watch what

00:54:21.910 --> 00:54:22.390
Rubinstein does.

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:24.990
Bishop f1, e3, get the knight to c5,

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:26.610
blockade the square, bring the rook to

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:29.850
c2, b4, fully ensconcing the rook on

00:54:30.070 --> 00:54:33.130
c5, and eventually Rubinstein has some tactics.

00:54:33.710 --> 00:54:35.110
The rule applies here as well.

00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:37.270
In this position, all of Rubinstein's

00:54:37.270 --> 00:54:38.510
pieces are in perfect squares.

00:54:38.790 --> 00:54:40.410
The bishop, the rook, the queen, the

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:41.410
other rook, and guess what?

00:54:41.470 --> 00:54:43.610
There is a tactic that involves rook

00:54:43.610 --> 00:54:45.690
takes c6 and queen takes a7.

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:48.250
At one moment, I'll invest more time in

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:49.750
this game because there's a lot to talk

00:54:49.750 --> 00:54:52.070
about, but for those watching on

00:54:52.070 --> 00:54:53.190
YouTube, this has been a six -hour

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:56.050
stream, and I'm basically exhausted

00:54:56.050 --> 00:54:58.270
already, so I'm going to leave that for

00:54:58.270 --> 00:54:59.610
another occasion.

00:54:59.810 --> 00:55:02.730
And the final thing to discuss is how

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:06.330
exactly we saw 96 and 94, because after

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:07.910
this move, I think everything was

00:55:07.910 --> 00:55:09.110
incredibly straightforward.

00:55:09.490 --> 00:55:10.870
Once we win the spawn, the game is

00:55:10.870 --> 00:55:11.930
over, let's be real.

00:55:12.410 --> 00:55:14.310
But how do you find moves like this?

00:55:14.410 --> 00:55:16.550
Well, one piece of advice is everything

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:18.550
is placed perfectly, look for tactics,

00:55:18.990 --> 00:55:21.330
but the other things I mentioned during

00:55:21.330 --> 00:55:21.730
the game.

00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:24.290
There's contact on the d -file between

00:55:24.290 --> 00:55:24.910
the rooks.

00:55:25.190 --> 00:55:27.170
Any contact between heavy pieces is

00:55:27.170 --> 00:55:29.390
automatically a source of tactics,

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:33.170
particularly because the rook on d6 is undefended.

00:55:33.430 --> 00:55:37.890
The other thing to remember is that you

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:41.190
should not get fixated on maintaining

00:55:41.190 --> 00:55:42.570
one particular pawn structure.

00:55:42.630 --> 00:55:45.330
You should not be afraid to alter the

00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:47.290
pawn structure your opponent has in

00:55:47.290 --> 00:55:50.590
order to shift the object of attack

00:55:50.590 --> 00:55:52.550
from one file to another, and this is

00:55:52.550 --> 00:55:53.030
the case here.

00:55:53.110 --> 00:55:54.670
You just have to consider this capture

00:55:54.670 --> 00:55:56.690
because it's obvious that you want to

00:55:56.690 --> 00:55:58.730
go after one of black's pawns.

00:55:58.830 --> 00:56:01.130
So seeing these two moves is just a

00:56:01.130 --> 00:56:04.010
matter of calculating the stuff that

00:56:04.010 --> 00:56:05.730
you're supposed to calculate, and then

00:56:05.730 --> 00:56:08.630
seeing 94 is a matter of being aware of

00:56:08.630 --> 00:56:10.070
the placement of the rooks, the fact

00:56:10.070 --> 00:56:12.110
that the rook on d6 is undefended, and

00:56:12.110 --> 00:56:15.390
ultimately 94 should come to mind more

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:16.830
often than not, which is not to say

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:18.150
you're going to see all of these moves,

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:21.590
but when you solve and you go through

00:56:21.590 --> 00:56:22.870
this process, you're just trying to

00:56:22.870 --> 00:56:24.630
raise the probability that you find

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:25.210
certain things.

00:56:25.410 --> 00:56:27.390
Knight g5, knight takes c6, and the

00:56:27.390 --> 00:56:30.790
rest I think is very evident without commentary.

00:56:31.510 --> 00:56:34.030
We got our bishop back to b3 to attack d5.

00:56:34.210 --> 00:56:37.070
We played the very patient move c3, and

00:56:37.070 --> 00:56:38.950
then we pounced on the opportunity to

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:39.810
win the knight on e7.

00:56:40.050 --> 00:56:42.430
We didn't tunnel vision on the d5 pawn.

00:56:42.550 --> 00:56:44.350
This, by the way, I thought was a

00:56:44.350 --> 00:56:46.090
little bit more messy because the

00:56:46.090 --> 00:56:47.570
knight on e6 is pinned to the rook.

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:48.990
The engine indicates that it's

00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:50.150
completely winning with rook e4.

00:56:50.250 --> 00:56:52.010
I didn't see this move, but why?

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:54.430
Why bother when we can make a patient

00:56:54.430 --> 00:56:56.830
move that keeps all of our advantages

00:56:56.830 --> 00:57:00.570
and reserves the right to take on d5

00:57:00.570 --> 00:57:01.790
whenever we want to?

00:57:02.590 --> 00:57:04.290
Yeah, some of you were asking about

00:57:04.290 --> 00:57:05.210
bishop takes d5.

00:57:05.770 --> 00:57:08.370
So again, hopefully the object of my

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:09.210
worry is obvious.

00:57:09.370 --> 00:57:11.750
I also, for some reason, was a little

00:57:11.750 --> 00:57:12.790
concerned about knight d8.

00:57:12.830 --> 00:57:14.730
Of course, I missed from a distance

00:57:14.730 --> 00:57:16.650
that you have knight c7 check, which is

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:19.730
a simple discovered attack, but if I

00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:21.770
had seen rook to e4, I would have

00:57:21.770 --> 00:57:23.250
probably gone for this because this is

00:57:23.250 --> 00:57:25.230
like concretely unbeatable.

00:57:25.790 --> 00:57:28.030
Black can't even go rook e8 because

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:30.970
again there's knight c7, again, and you

00:57:30.970 --> 00:57:32.030
win the rook in a different way.

00:57:33.010 --> 00:57:34.550
So this is just two pawns up.

00:57:34.790 --> 00:57:37.770
Also from the corner of my eye, I

00:57:37.770 --> 00:57:39.770
thought maybe this move is going to be

00:57:39.770 --> 00:57:41.450
annoying, but you can just drop the

00:57:41.450 --> 00:57:43.170
rook back to d2, and then again you can

00:57:43.170 --> 00:57:45.590
play rook e4 on the next move.

00:57:46.690 --> 00:57:49.570
All right guys, I think everything is clear.

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:51.810
So what we had was a summary of the

00:57:51.810 --> 00:57:53.290
theory after f3.

00:57:53.410 --> 00:57:55.150
Remember, queen b6 is the best move.

00:57:55.390 --> 00:57:57.270
Still doesn't solve all of black's problems.

00:57:57.630 --> 00:57:58.810
e5 happened in the game.

00:57:58.890 --> 00:58:00.930
D takes e4 is what I spent the most

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:01.410
time on.

00:58:02.110 --> 00:58:04.490
Then we could have played bishop d4 in

00:58:04.490 --> 00:58:06.910
this to immediately target black's

00:58:06.910 --> 00:58:07.530
strongest piece.

00:58:07.610 --> 00:58:09.390
This was a sub -theme of the game,

00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:11.430
targeting your opponent's strongest pieces.

00:58:11.550 --> 00:58:13.070
The other main theme was patience, right?

00:58:13.490 --> 00:58:14.770
This game was all about patience.

00:58:15.210 --> 00:58:17.490
It started here, where knight takes d5,

00:58:17.770 --> 00:58:19.990
super impatient move, would allow

00:58:19.990 --> 00:58:22.590
either a big lead in development or

00:58:22.590 --> 00:58:25.270
chaos after bishop takes b2, neither of

00:58:25.270 --> 00:58:25.810
which we want.

00:58:26.170 --> 00:58:28.510
So we patiently develop our queenside.

00:58:28.570 --> 00:58:31.030
Then patience in this part of the game

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:32.790
where we just bring our pieces and put

00:58:32.790 --> 00:58:34.410
them on good squares because we have

00:58:34.410 --> 00:58:35.230
the time to do that.

00:58:35.610 --> 00:58:36.890
Trading off your opponent's strongest

00:58:36.890 --> 00:58:39.970
piece, rook he1, and then tactical

00:58:39.970 --> 00:58:41.870
awareness at the right moments, right?

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:43.550
Understanding that when everything is

00:58:43.550 --> 00:58:46.150
well placed, you're likely to have some

00:58:46.150 --> 00:58:48.390
way to convert the positional advantage

00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:49.970
into something more tactical.

00:58:50.130 --> 00:58:51.610
That's what I was able to do by

00:58:51.610 --> 00:58:53.030
bringing the knight to g5, winning the

00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:54.670
pawn, and the technical phase of the

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:56.090
game was very straightforward.

00:58:56.690 --> 00:58:57.770
Thanks for hanging out, everybody!

